    "summary": "Epic Bird Song, soaring Other One and tight jam‑packed set",
    "blurb": "The first set rocks with highlights like Bird Song, Box of Rain and Me And My Uncle, then the second set erupts into a high‑energy stretch featuring The Other One, He's Gone, Truckin' and an extended Brokedown Palace jam. Jerry’s guitar is scorching and Keith adds jazzy piano flourishes throughout. The recommended soundboard capture is crisp with excellent instrument separation.",
    "review": "On November 17, 1972 at Century II Convention Hall in Wichita, the Dead turned in a performance that still feels fresh decades later. Set one opens solidly with classics such as The Promised Land and Sugaree before launching into a radiant Bird Song where Bob’s vocal shines and Jerry delivers scorching lead on Me And My Uncle. Box of Rain is rendered with studio‑like clarity, showcasing Phil’s warm vocal tone. The second set is where the magic truly unfolds: a blistering Cumberland Blues kicks off a soaring sequence of He's Gone → Truckin' → Jam → The Other One that builds into an expansive, jazzy excursion before flowing seamlessly into Brokedown Palace and a jubilant Sugar Magnolia. Keith Godchaux’s piano work adds a sophisticated jazz edge to The Other One, while the rhythm section holds everything together with steady drive.\n\nBoth available soundboard recordings are praised for their clarity; the Lai‑transferred SBD (gd1972-11-17.sbd.lai.1185.shnf) is noted as “crisp” and “excellent,” only marred by a few minor speed quirks, making it the preferred listen. The show’s energy never wanes, and despite a weak Jack Straw that slips under the radar, the overall performance stands out as one of the year’s finest showcases of the late‑1972 era.",
